nina (docs): Opening a quick coordination thread for the SDK snippets PR that converts old sync streaming examples to the new async iterator + pagination + retry guidance. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/4420 — PTAL SDK folks for polyglot parity (py/ts/go).

raj (sdk): on it. plan: keep a tiny compatibility note for anyone still on sync calls but make the main examples async-first. will send code blocks in this thread.

kevin (eng-runtime): FYI: runtime change rolled out yesterday: on a retried streaming session we now emit a new Retry-Token and invalidate any per-request prefix cache entries (this prevents stale KV hits). docs should mention handling Retry-Token + re-syncing cursor state.

nina (docs): good catch. can you suggest one clear sentence we can paste?

kevin (eng-runtime): suggested copy: "When a stream is automatically retried, the service issues a new Retry-Token and clears per-request KV prefix entries. To resume pagination correctly, re-fetch the cursor using the Retry-Token and continue from the server-provided marker." short and prescriptive.

anya (pm): makes sense. customers will want a short migration checklist too — maybe 3 bullets: detect retry, fetch cursor, resume.

raj (sdk): here's a compact python snippet for the new examples—async-first, uses public APIs, shows RetryToken handling:

```python
# py_paginate_retry.py
from redwood import Client, TransientError

client = Client()

async def stream_pages(prompt: str):
    async for stream in client.stream(model="open-3a", input=prompt):
        try:
            async for page in stream.pages():
                yield page
        except TransientError as err:
            # stream was retried: get server cursor using retry token
            retry_token = getattr(stream, "retry_token", None)
            cursor = await client.cursor_for_retry(retry_token)
            async for page in client.resume_pages(cursor):
                yield page
```

nina (docs): nice and concise. can you rename cursor_for_retry to get_cursor_from_retry for clarity? keeps public API explicit.

raj (sdk): updated snippet name in the PR. now TS sample — async iterator + helper wrapper that hides cursor re-fetch: a short pattern for docs and GitHub gist.

```ts
// ts_paginate_retry.ts
import RedwoodClient from '@redwood/inference'
const client = new RedwoodClient()

export async function* pagesWithRetry(prompt: string) {
  for await (const s of client.streamResponses({ model: 'open-3a', input: prompt })) {
    try {
      for await (const p of s.paginate()) {
        yield p
      }
    } catch (err) {
      // on retry, get cursor via retry token and resume
      const token = (s as any).retryToken
      const cursor = await client.getCursorFromRetry(token)
      for await (const p of client.resumePages(cursor)) {
        yield p
      }
    }
  }
}
```

kevin (eng-runtime): TS looks fine. small suggestion: docs should warn that retryToken may be absent for manual retries — add fallback to full re-query.

raj (sdk): will add a one-line fallback comment in TS. now Go example — using ResumeStream(cursor) pattern (Go SDK doesn't expose resumePages):

```go
// go_paginate_retry.go
package examples

import (
  "context"
  rw "github.com/redwood-inference/go-sdk"
)

func StreamPagesWithRetry(ctx context.Context, prompt string) <-chan rw.Page {
  out := make(chan rw.Page)
  go func() {
    defer close(out)
    client := rw.NewClient()
    stream, _ := client.StreamResponses(ctx, rw.StreamParams{Model: "open-3a", Input: prompt})
    for stream.Next() {
      pages, err := stream.Pages()
      if err != nil {
        // retry path: obtain token and resume a new stream
        token, _ := stream.RetryToken()
        cursor, _ := client.GetCursorFromRetry(ctx, token)
        resumed := client.ResumeStream(ctx, cursor)
        for resumed.Next() {
          for _, p := range resumed.Pages() {
            out <- p
          }
        }
        return
      }
      for _, p := range pages {
        out <- p
      }
    }
  }()
  return out
}
```

